---
import GuideLayout from '../layouts/GuideLayout.astro';
import Verse from '../components/Verse.astro';

const faqs = [
  {
    q: 'What is the best Bible verse for sleep?',
    a: 'Psalm 4:8 is the verse most people turn to: “I will both lay me down in peace, and sleep: for thou, LORD, only makest me dwell in safety.” It was written by David at the end of a hard day, and it ties sleep directly to trust rather than to circumstances.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Which Bible verses help with anxiety at night?',
    a: 'Philippians 4:6–7, 1 Peter 5:7, Isaiah 26:3 and John 14:27 all speak to a worried mind. Philippians 4:6–7 is especially practical: it turns worry into prayer and promises a peace that keeps the heart and mind.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Is there a Bible verse for sleepless nights?',
    a: 'Psalm 63:6–7 describes lying awake and remembering God “in the night watches”. It treats wakefulness as a time for quiet prayer rather than frustration, which many people find easier than forcing sleep.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Why are these verses in the King James Version?',
    a: 'The King James Version is the translation used for the Daily Scripture passages in the Bible Bedtimes app, and its rhythm suits reading aloud slowly at night.',
  },
];
---
<GuideLayout
  title="18 Bible Verses for Sleep (KJV) — Peace, Rest and Anxious Nights | Bible Bedtime"
  description="Bible verses for sleep in the King James Version: verses for peace, anxiety at night, protection and sleepless nights, with a simple way to use them at bedtime."
  eyebrow="Scripture for bedtime"
  heading="18 Bible verses for sleep"
  intro="King James verses to read slowly at the end of the day: for lying down in peace, for a mind that will not settle, for protection through the night, and for the hours when sleep will not come."
  crumb="Bible verses for sleep"
  campaign="site_guide_verses_sleep"
  faqs={faqs}
>
  <p>Scripture does not promise that every night will be easy. It does keep returning to one idea: rest is something God gives, not something we earn by getting everything finished. These verses are grouped by the kind of night you are having, so you can go straight to the one you need.</p>

  <h2>Verses for lying down in peace</h2>
  <p>Start here on an ordinary evening. Each of these links sleep to trust.</p>
  <Verse ref="Psalms 4:8" />
  <p>David wrote this after a day of pressure and criticism. The peace comes before the sleep, and the safety comes from God alone.</p>
  <Verse ref="Proverbs 3:24" />
  <Verse ref="Psalms 127:2" />
  <p>A gentle correction for anyone who works late to stay ahead: sleep is described as a gift to the people God loves.</p>
  <Verse ref="Psalms 3:5" />

  <h2>Verses for an anxious mind</h2>
  <p>When thoughts keep circling, these verses give the worry somewhere to go.</p>
  <Verse ref="Philippians 4:6-7" />
  <p>“Be careful for nothing” means “do not be anxious about anything”. Paul’s answer is not to ignore the worry but to pray it, with thanks, and let God’s peace stand guard.</p>
  <Verse ref="1 Peter 5:7" />
  <Verse ref="Isaiah 26:3" />
  <Verse ref="John 14:27" />
  <Verse ref="Matthew 11:28-30" />
  <p>Jesus’ invitation is to the tired and the overloaded, and what he offers is rest for the soul, not just the body.</p>
  <Verse ref="Psalms 56:3" />

  <h2>Verses for protection through the night</h2>
  <Verse ref="Psalms 91:1-2" />
  <Verse ref="Psalms 121:3-4" />
  <p>The one who keeps you does not sleep, so you can. That is what makes Psalm 121 such a natural bedtime psalm.</p>
  <Verse ref="Deuteronomy 33:12" />

  <h2>Verses for sleepless nights</h2>
  <p>If you are awake at 3am, these verses change the question from “why can’t I sleep?” to “who is with me now?”</p>
  <Verse ref="Psalms 63:6-7" />
  <Verse ref="Psalms 16:7-8" />
  <Verse ref="Psalms 42:8" />
  <Verse ref="Psalms 46:10" />

  <h2>And one for the morning</h2>
  <Verse ref="Lamentations 3:22-23" />

  <h2>How to use Bible verses at bedtime</h2>
  <ul class="bullets">
    <li><strong>Choose one verse, not ten.</strong> A single verse read slowly two or three times settles the mind better than a long list.</li>
    <li><strong>Read it aloud, quietly.</strong> Hearing the words slows your breathing and keeps your attention from drifting back to the day.</li>
    <li><strong>Pray it back.</strong> Turn the verse into a sentence to God, for example: “Lord, I will lie down in peace, because you keep me safe.”</li>
    <li><strong>Keep it by the bed.</strong> If you wake in the night, you will not have to reach for a bright screen to find it.</li>
    <li><strong>Let someone else read.</strong> Listening to Scripture read calmly, as in the <a class="inline" href="/bible-sleep-stories/">Bible sleep stories</a> in our app, lets you close your eyes while the words carry on.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>If you would like a longer passage, the <a class="inline" href="/psalms-for-sleep/">psalms for sleep</a> are a natural next step, and our <a class="inline" href="/bedtime-prayers-for-adults/">bedtime prayers for adults</a> are written to follow them.</p>
</GuideLayout>
